course: dictionary
lesson: 12508
title: A depositor puts money safely away
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A depositor is a person who puts money into a bank account. The word comes from deposit, which means to put something down to keep it safe. «Maya is a depositor at the Riverside Bank, and she puts her birthday money in every month.» Maya is the one who puts the money in, so she is the depositor.
explain: Look at the ending -or. It often shows a person who does something: a visitor visits, an actor acts, and a depositor deposits. When you meet depositor, think: who is putting something in safe keeping?
explain: News stories use this word too. «When the bank had problems, the depositors rushed to take out their savings.» The depositors are the people whose money is in the bank, and they worry about getting it back.
fact: A depositor is a person who puts money into a bank account.
fact: Depositor comes from the verb deposit, which means to put something down for safe keeping.
fact: «Bank depositors» are the people whose money is kept in a bank.
fact: «Protect depositors» means keep the people who saved money safe from losing it.
fact: The ending -or often shows a person, as in visitor, actor and depositor.
exercise: «Ella put her birthday money into the bank every week.» Who is the depositor? | *Ella | The birthday money | The week | The bank
exercise: What does depositor mean? | A bank worker who counts the coins | A bank that has closed for good | *A person who puts money into a bank account | A coin that has been lost
exercise: «When the bank was in trouble, the depositors rushed to take out their savings.» What did the depositors do? | Ran a race to the door | *Took their money out | Built a new bank | Put more money in
test: Which word means a person who puts money into a bank? | *depositor | borrower | bank manager | cashier
test: In depositor, which ending tells you it is a person? | -ed | -ly | -ing | *-or
exam: A bank promises to «protect depositors». What does protect mean here? | *Keep safe from harm or loss | Close the door on | Count out loud | Hit or push
